<!--
  loweredswitchvalue (GH-468): a re-rolled lowered switch must dispatch on the
  value its cascade compared, or be withdrawn.

  `loweredswitch` detects a comparison cascade on the simplified SSA graph, where
  every compare reads one value, and records that value's home storage. The
  install re-lifts raw p-code on the restart and points the synthesized BRANCHIND
  at storage at the cascade head, and that storage need not hold the value
  there.

  Bytes: `dispatch` @ 0xd380 is gcc -O2's tail-call dispatcher on its first
  parameter, from a real x86-64 ELF (kuna-re-dataset bin_213). It compiles the
  argument once (`mov eax,edi`) and reuses EDI (the first-parameter register) for
  the tail calls' own argument (`mov rdi,rsi`) before the compares run, so EDI -
  the recorded home storage, holds the SECOND parameter by the time the switch
  would read it. The tail-call targets are stubbed `ret`.

  Pass 1 (option loweredswitchvalue off): the switch reads EDI at the head and
  dispatches on the second parameter (`v1 = (unsigned int)a1; switch(v1)`), which
  the disassembly never does.
  Pass 2 (default, on): the install reads EAX, what `cmp eax,c` reads, and the
  restart proves the BRANCHIND input is the first parameter, so it dispatches on
  the compared value and keeps it in the prototype.

  Bytes: `big_consts` @ 0x401060, gcc 11 -O2 over
  `void big_consts(long x, long a) { hv(a); switch (x) { case 0x123456789: ... } hv(a); }`
  with `noipa` callees. x moves to RBX and RDI carries a to the first call; each
  compare loads its 64-bit constant into RAX (`movabs rax,C ; cmp rbx,rax`), so the
  head compare reads two registers and has no single operand. The install falls
  back to RDI, the restart names what RDI holds there (the value `hv` leaves) as a
  different value, and the switch is withdrawn.
  Pass 1 (off): the switch dispatches on the second parameter (`v1 = a1;
  switch(v1)`).
  Pass 2 (on): no switch; the compare tree over a0 is kept.
-->
